00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.210
The ability of Python to allow you to use broadcasting operations and more generally,

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:11.810
the great flexibility of the Python NumPy programming language is, I think, both a strength

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:14.810
as well as a weakness of the programming language.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:19.210
I think it's a strength because the great expressivity of the language, the great flexibility

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.250
of the language lets you get a lot done with even just a single line of code.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:30.010
But it's also a weakness because with broadcasting and this great amount of flexibility, sometimes

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.490
it's possible you can introduce very subtle bugs or very strange looking bugs if you aren't

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:38.730
familiar with all of the intricacies of how broadcasting and how features like broadcasting

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:39.730
work.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:44.810
For example, if you take a column vector and add it to a row vector, you might expect

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:48.770
it to throw up a dimension mismatch or type error or something.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:54.050
But you might actually get back a matrix as a sum of a row vector and a column vector.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:59.210
So there is an internal logic to these strange effects of Python, but if you aren't familiar

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:05.690
with Python, now I've seen some students have very strange, very hard to find bugs.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:09.890
So what I want to do in this video is share with you a couple of tips and tricks that

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:15.570
have been very useful for me to eliminate or simplify and eliminate a lot of the strange

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.050
looking bugs in my own code.

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:22.210
And I hope that with these tips and tricks, you also be able to much more easily write

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.370
bug three Python and NumPy code.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:31.690
To illustrate one of the less intuitive effects of Python NumPy, especially how you construct

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.770
vectors in Python NumPy, let me do a quick demo.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:42.530
Let's say I set a equals np.random.randn 5.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:49.330
So this creates a five random Gaussian variables stored in array a.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:52.370
And so let's print a.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:02:02.730
Now it turns out that the shape of a when you do this is this five calmer structure.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:08.210
And so this is called a rank one array in Python, and it's neither a row vector nor

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:09.770
a column vector.

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.570
And this leads us to have some slightly nonintuitive effect.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:17.210
So for example, if I print a transpose, it ends up looking the same as a.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.250
So a and a transpose end up looking the same.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:26.450
And if I print the inner product between a and a transpose, you might think a times a

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.210
transpose is maybe the outer product should give you a matrix maybe.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:34.250
But if I do that, you instead get back a number.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:39.530
So what I would recommend is that when you're coding neural networks, that you just not

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:45.330
use data structures, where the shape is this, you know, five calmer n calmer, or rank one

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.330
array.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:57.880
Instead, if you set a to be this five by one, then this commits a to be a five by one column

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.080
vector.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:03.680
And whereas previously a and a transpose look the same if you now print a transpose.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.800
Now a transpose is a row vector.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.520
Notice one subtle difference.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.360
In this data structure, there are two square brackets when you print a transpose, whereas

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.480
previously there was one square bracket.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:21.360
So that's the difference between a this is really a one by five matrix versus a one of

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.640
these are rank one arrays.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:30.160
And if you print, say the product between a and a transpose, then this gives you the

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.400
outer product of a vector, right?

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.800
And so the outer product of a vector gives you a matrix.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:40.280
So let's look in greater detail at what we just saw here.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:46.960
The first command that we ran just now was this, and this created a data structure where

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:52.520
a dot shape was this funny thing five calmer.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:57.280
And so this is called a rank one array.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.200
And this is a very funny data structure, it doesn't behave consistently as either a row

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.780
vector nor a column vector, which makes some of its effects non intuitive.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.500
So what I'm going to recommend is that when you're doing your program exercises, or in

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:17.220
fact, when you're implementing logistic regression on your networks, that you just do not use

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.140
these rank one arrays.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:27.260
Instead, if every time you create an array, you commit to making it either a column vector,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:32.860
so this creates a five or one vector or commit to making it a row vector, then the behavior

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:36.760
of your vectors will maybe easier to understand.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:43.280
So in this case, a dot shape is going to be equal to five comma one.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.540
And so this behaves a lot like a, that this is a column vector.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.280
And that's why you might you can think of this as a five by one matrix or as a column

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:53.400
vector.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:05:00.040
And here, a dot shape is going to be one comma five, and this behaves consistently as

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.160
a row vector.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:08.240
So when you need a vector, I would say either use this or this, but not a rank one array.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.240
One more thing that I do a lot in my code is if I'm not entirely sure what's the dimension

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:18.480
of one of my vectors, I'll often throw in an assertion statement like this to make sure

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.720
in this case that this is a five by one vector.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.760
So this is a column vector.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:29.560
These assertions are really inexpensive to execute, and they also help to serve as documentation

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:30.560
of your code.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.560
So don't hesitate to throw in assertion statements like this one if you feel like it.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:40.920
And then finally, if for some reason you do end up with a rank one array, you can reshape

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:49.080
this a equals a dot reshape into, say a five by one array or one by five array so that

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:53.760
it behaves more consistently as either a column vector or row vector.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.120
So sometimes students end up with very hard to track down bugs because of some of the

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:01.200
nonintuitive effects of rank one arrays.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.760
By eliminating rank one arrays on my own code, I think my code became simpler, and I did

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:10.520
not actually find it restrictive in terms of things I could express in code to just

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:12.800
never use a rank one array.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:18.240
And so takeaways are to simplify your code, don't use rank one arrays, always use either

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:24.520
n by one matrices, basically column vectors, or one by n matrices, or basically row vectors.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.480
Be free to toss a lot of assertion statements to double check the dimensions of your matrices

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:29.600
and arrays.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.360
And also, you know, don't be shy about calling the reshape operation to make sure that your

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.800
matrices or your vectors are the dimension that you need it to be.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.520
So that I hope that this set of suggestions helps you to eliminate a class of bugs that

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.800
we have from Python code and makes a programming exercise easier for you to complete.
